[68.34935, "i", "cat > /home/user/scripts/CLEANUP_FIX_SUMMARY.txt << 'EOF'\nCLEANUP SCRIPT FIX SUMMARY\n==========================\n\nPROBLEM:\nThe cleanup.sh script was either deleting nothing or deleting too many files.\n\nROOT CAUSES:\n1. Filename handling with spaces: The script used 'while read' without proper\n   IFS handling, causing filenames with spaces to be split incorrectly.\n\n2. Broken pipe architecture: The original script piped 'while read' output to\n   'xargs rm -rf', but this doesn't work reliably because:\n   - The while loop runs in a subshell due to the pipe\n   - xargs may receive incomplete or corrupted input\n   - No null-termination meant spaces broke the command\n\n3. No debug output: Made it impossible to see what was actually happening.\n\nFIXES APPLIED:\n1. Added '-print0' to find and 'IFS= read -r -d \"\"' to handle filenames with\n   spaces, parentheses, brackets, and other special characters safely.\n\n2. Moved 'rm -rf' inside the while loop instead of piping to xargs, ensuring\n   each deletion happens with the correc"]
[68.349488, "i", "t filename.\n\n3. Added clear debug output showing what's being kept vs deleted and why.\n\nRESULTS:\n- Script now correctly identifies .dSYM bundles older than 7 days\n- Only deletes those WITHOUT matching .ipa files\n- Preserves .dSYM bundles that have active releases (.ipa files)\n- Handles all special characters in filenames correctly\n\nTESTED: Successfully deleted 3 old .dSYM bundles without .ipa files while\npreserving 2 .dSYM bundles with matching .ipa files.\nEOF\n"]
[68.349588, "o", "cat > /home/user/scripts/CLEANUP_FIX_SUMMARY.txt << 'EOF'\r\n"]
[68.349609, "o", "\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[68.349662, "o", "\u001b[?2004h> "]
[68.349746, "o", "CLEANUP SCRIPT FIX SUMMARY\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
